## Screenshots / notes for reviewers

Data sources: the cache (loc 12982: size 1x2, blocks walking); RSProx recording 1718 (revision 227), ticks 2328-2333: the player reaches the in-line tile outside the stile on the op tick, steps onto the near footprint tile the next tick, climbs (seq 839 delay 30, exactmove 30/94 to the far footprint tile) the tick after that, and steps off onto the tile beyond three ticks later. As a 2004 reference only, LostCity's content scripts (MIT, https://github.com/LostCityRS/Content, `skill_agility/scripts/shortcuts.rs2`) cross the same way.

Caveats:
- No `loc_merge`: the official game merges the player with the stile model during the climb (start 30, end 94); the engine has no loc-merge API, so the model isn't merged (visual only).
- Clicking from beside the stile or from a diagonal tile: the recording only shows an in-line approach. We walk the player to the in-line tile first (a guess).
- The step on and step off are one-tile non-jump teleports; they go out as walk steps, as in the recording. The climb's own tile change goes out as a teleport (the recording: a walk step); that comes from `exactMove`, which the stile already used.
- Clicking from beside the stile, the walk to the in-line tile starts the tick after the interaction; standing on the in-line tile already, the step on comes on the interaction's tick. The recording shows neither case.

Checklist notes:
- Tests only cover complex logic and anything that moves items: no new tests, this is movement timing and geometry
- State in varbits/varps, not attrs: no state
- Matches the OSRS wiki: the deviations are in the caveats above, not in `docs/quirks.md`
- New content checked against rsprox dumps where possible: recording 1718, the pen stile crossed from the north

